```json
{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Applying Best-of-N candidate selection with a learned value/reward model to frozen robot policies is well-established in adjacent work: RoVer (arXiv 2510.10975) does candidate generation + PRM scoring + argmax for frozen VLAs (CALVIN benchmark); GPC-RANK does hard argmax over action proposals ranked by a separately trained world model; and Verifier-free Test-Time Sampling (ICLR 2026, arXiv 2510.05681) implements Best-of-N without additional training for VLAs. Applying this paradigm specifically to WAM action latents (UWM action-only mode, hard argmax, diversity pilot) is the differentiating engineering contribution, but the core concept is incremental relative to this body of work — and none of these papers are cited by the Generator."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The ≤40 H100-hours compute budget is correctly scoped for training; the sequential dependency (Step 0 failure collection → diversity pilot → MLP training → evaluation) compresses the critical path to ~12-15 days, making the 20-day CoRL deadline feasible only with continuous automation. The central feasibility risk is the frozen UWM baseline on RoboCasa: no published results exist for UWM evaluated on RoboCasa without task-specific fine-tuning, and the Generator's 40-55% baseline estimate is unvalidated — if the actual figure is 15-30% (plausible given RoboCasa's kitchen-task distribution), the ≥60% target requires a +30-45pp lift that a 3-layer MLP scoring action latents is very unlikely to achieve."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Round 3 is the clearest version yet: hard argmax is now the primary result with explicit justification, Step 0 (failure collection) is fully specified, the diversity pilot is well-defined with concrete diagnostic thresholds, and the Fast-WAM complementarity framing is sharp. The proposal has clear ablation structure and well-scoped evaluation targets."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "If the ≥60% RoboCasa claim is validated, demonstrating near fine-tuned performance at 40× lower training cost would be impactful for the WAM community; but the contribution's impact is bounded by the narrow architectural scope (UWM + DreamZero) and the lack of real-robot experiments, and the incremental novelty relative to RoVer/GPC limits expected CoRL-reviewer enthusiasm."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "SIGNIFICANT — Three important related papers are completely absent from the landscape: (1) RoVer: Robot Reward Model as Test-Time Verifier for Vision-Language-Action Models (arXiv 2510.10975): generates B candidate actions from a frozen VLA, scores with a trained Process Reward Model (PRM), and selects via argmax — evaluated on CALVIN + real robot (GR-1, Dita, MoDE backbones). Different policy class (VLA not WAM) and benchmark (CALVIN not LIBERO/RoboCasa), but the paradigm is essentially identical. Must be cited and differentiated. (2) Verifier-free Test-Time Sampling for Vision Language Action Models (arXiv 2510.05681, ICLR 2026): Best-of-N for frozen VLAs using KL-divergence from a masked reference distribution as a confidence signal — no additional training required; achieves 168% relative improvement on pick-and-place. Differentiator: no trained value model; applicable to VLAs not WAMs. Must be cited. (3) Compose Your Policies! (arXiv 2510.01068): test-time policy composition for diffusion/flow-based robot policies without retraining, using score composition. Must be cited.",
    "CRITICAL — Frozen UWM baseline on RoboCasa is an unvalidated assumption that may be dramatically wrong. The UWM paper (RSS 2025, arXiv 2504.02792) evaluates primarily on LIBERO and OXE-distribution tasks; no published RoboCasa-without-fine-tuning results exist. Cosmos Policy achieves 67.1% on RoboCasa only after 1,536 H100-hours of domain-specific fine-tuning. A frozen UWM — not fine-tuned on RoboCasa's kitchen tasks — achieving 40-55% baseline success seems optimistic; 15-30% is more plausible. If the actual baseline is 15-30%, the ≥60% target requires +30-45pp from a 3-layer MLP scoring action latents, which is almost certainly too large. The Generator should: (a) run a quick 50-episode RoboCasa baseline with frozen UWM before committing to ≥60% target; (b) recalibrate the target to 'improve baseline by ≥10pp' rather than 'approach Cosmos Policy 67.1%' if baseline is lower than expected.",
    "SIGNIFICANT — The World-Value-Action (WAV) model (arXiv 2604.14732, April 2026) proposes a trajectory value function that 'evaluates long-horizon utility' and generates actions via 'inference in latent space where the model progressively concentrates probability mass on high-value trajectories.' This is substantially overlapping in motivation (value scoring over latent trajectory representations) though architecturally different (implicit latent inference vs. discrete Best-of-N selection). Should be cited and explicitly differentiated: WAV modifies the VLA architecture; this proposal keeps all weights frozen.",
    "MODERATE — Novelty argument is insufficiently grounded for CoRL acceptance. The technical differentiation from RoVer reduces to: (a) WAM action latents vs. VLA action space, (b) UWM action-only mode vs. standard policy, (c) LIBERO/RoboCasa vs. CALVIN. These are valid engineering differences but unlikely to meet CoRL's bar for conceptual novelty. The proposal needs a sharper 'why WAM action latents?' argument — specifically, that the WAM's joint video-action pretraining instills physical grounding into the action latent z_a that pure action-space policies lack, making the latent richer for value estimation. This would differentiate from RoVer on principled grounds, not just implementation.",
    "MODERATE — The DreamZero-14B cross-WAM evaluation is described as secondary but is essential for the 'plug-in across WAM architectures' claim. DreamZero-14B requires full video+action denoising (no native action-only mode), making B-trajectory sampling substantially more expensive than for UWM. If this experiment cannot be completed within the 20-day window (likely, given 50 H100-hours estimate), the 'plug-in' contribution collapses to a single-architecture result, significantly weakening the generalization claim. The Generator should either (a) commit to completing DreamZero validation within the timeline or (b) reframe the contribution as UWM-specific and drop the plug-in generalization claim.",
    "MODERATE — The feasibility of training a discriminative value MLP from action latents depends on a premise not yet verified: that frozen UWM action latents from successful and failed demonstrations are separable in the latent space. If UWM's action latent z_a primarily encodes task semantics (what to do) rather than execution quality (how well to do it), success/failure labels may be uncorrelated with latent structure and the MLP will learn a degenerate classifier. The diversity pilot (Spearman ρ > 0.3 target) partially addresses this, but binary classification accuracy on held-out demos should also be reported as a sanity check before the main ablation."
  ],
  "feedback": "The Round 3 revision has correctly addressed all three Round 2 critical issues: (1) hard argmax is now the primary result with solid justification, (2) Step 0 failure collection is explicitly specified at 15-20 H100-hours, and (3) Fast-WAM is now integrated as a complementary framing. The diversity pilot is a strong addition. However, three concrete fixes are needed before CoRL submission:\n\n**1. Add and differentiate three missing related papers (1-2 days).** You must cite RoVer (arXiv 2510.10975), Verifier-free Test-Time Sampling (ICLR 2026, arXiv 2510.05681), and Compose Your Policies! (arXiv 2510.01068) in your related work section. The differentiation argument for RoVer specifically should be: 'RoVer applies to VLA architectures in token-action space using an explicitly trained PRM; our method targets WAMs whose joint video-action pretraining produces physically grounded action latents z_a that are richer substrates for value estimation than action tokens from VLAs — we exploit this WAM-specific property to eliminate the need for a separate process reward model training stage.' Also cite WAV (arXiv 2604.14732) as addressing value-guided action selection within VLAs via implicit latent inference, and differentiate: 'WAV modifies the architecture; Frozen-WAM BoN leaves all weights frozen.'\n\n**2. Validate the frozen UWM baseline on RoboCasa before committing to ≥60% target (3-5 days, highest priority).** Run 50 episodes of B=1 frozen UWM action-only inference on a representative subset of RoboCasa tasks. If the baseline is 40-55%, the ≥60% target is appropriate. If it is 15-35% (which is equally plausible given no published UWM/RoboCasa results), you must: (a) adjust the target to 'improve baseline success rate by ≥10pp absolute,' and (b) reframe the comparison away from 'approaching fine-tuned Cosmos Policy (67.1%)' toward 'meaningful improvement over frozen WAM without any fine-tuning, at ≤40 H100-hours training.' The latter is still a publishable result; the former collapses if the baseline is too low.\n\n**3. Sharpen the theoretical motivation for WAM latents over action space (0.5 days).** Add one paragraph explaining why z_a from a jointly-trained video-action WAM contains more quality-discriminative information than the output action tokens of a VLA. The key insight: UWM's video diffusion process regularizes z_a to be consistent with physically plausible visual futures, instilling motion-physics grounding that raw VLA action tokens lack. This is why a 3-layer MLP over z_a can succeed where the same MLP over VLA output tokens would fail — and this is your fundamental novelty claim that differentiates Frozen-WAM BoN from RoVer at the conceptual level.\n\nMinimum viable CoRL submission: frozen UWM + RoboCasa Best-of-N (primary) + LIBERO-Long (secondary) + diversity pilot figure + B/hard-argmax ablation. Drop DreamZero cross-WAM to camera-ready — it adds a 50 H100-hour sequential dependency that cannot be completed before the diversity pilot results inform whether the premise holds.",
  "reason": "Significant Round 3 improvements executed correctly, but missing three directly relevant papers (RoVer, Verifier-free TTS, Compose Your Policies!), an unvalidated RoboCasa baseline assumption that could invalidate the ≥60% target claim, and insufficient theoretical grounding for why WAM action latents outperform VLA action space for value learning — these three must be resolved before CoRL submission."
}
